    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## What goes wrong without uniform convergence\n\nThe exchange of limits fails:\n$$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\lim_{x\\to 1^-} f_n(x) = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} 1 = 1 \\;\\ne\\; \\lim_{x\\to 1^-} \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} f_n(x) = \\lim_{x\\to 1^-} 0 = 0.$$\n\nContinuity, integrability, and differentiability of the limit can all break down.\n\n## Theorem: uniform convergence preserves continuity\n\nIf each $f_n$ is continuous on $[a, b]$ and $f_n \\to f$ uniformly, then $f$ is continuous. Conversely, the $x^n$ counter-example shows uniformity matters: drop it, and the limit can become discontinuous.\n\n## Weierstrass M-test\n\nFor $\\sum f_n(x)$ on a domain, if there are constants $M_n$ with $|f_n(x)| \\le M_n$ for all $x$ and $\\sum M_n$ converges, then $\\sum f_n$ converges **uniformly** on the domain.\n\nApplication: $\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\cos(n x)/n^2$ on $\\mathbb{R}$. Bound: $|\\cos(nx)/n^2| \\le 1/n^2$; $\\sum 1/n^2 = \\pi^2/6$ converges; therefore the series converges uniformly to a continuous function on all of $\\mathbb{R}$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "g_uniform(x, N) := sum(cos(n * x) / n^2, n, 1, N)$\nx_g : np_to_list(np_linspace(-2*float(%pi), 2*float(%pi), 400))$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"lightcoral\", line_width=1, name=\"N=1 partial\",\n  lines(x_g, map(lambda([x], float(g_uniform(x, 1))), x_g)),\n  color=\"orange\", line_width=1, name=\"N=5 partial\",\n  lines(x_g, map(lambda([x], float(g_uniform(x, 5))), x_g)),\n  color=\"green\", line_width=1, name=\"N=20 partial\",\n  lines(x_g, map(lambda([x], float(g_uniform(x, 20))), x_g)),\n  color=\"blue\", line_width=2, name=\"N=100 partial (≈ uniform limit)\",\n  lines(x_g, map(lambda([x], float(g_uniform(x, 100))), x_g)),\n  title=\"Σ cos(nx)/n² — uniform convergence by Weierstrass M-test\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"partial sum\",\n  grid=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 31,
      "outputs": [
        {
